1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,410
Picture an investor's phone lighting up:

2
00:00:02,420 --> 00:00:04,960
a payment, right on schedule, like always.

3
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:09,189
Months later, a different message:
a delay on the platform side,

4
00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:11,300
and the money should arrive next week.

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,757
That message is a reconstruction.
The case behind it is real,

6
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:17,580
and it is on the public record.

7
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:22,240
The man at the center of
it was Zachary Horwitz,

8
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:25,750
an actor in Los Angeles who
used the screen name Zach Avery.

9
00:00:26,670 --> 00:00:31,091
His company issued promissory notes
that typically came due in 6 or 12

10
00:00:31,101 --> 00:00:34,330
months, with a promised
return of 25 to 45 percent.

11
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:39,242
The money, he told investors, would
buy film rights to be licensed to

12
00:00:39,252 --> 00:00:41,830
streaming platforms such as HBO and Netflix.

13
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:46,270
In his plea agreement, he admitted
the distribution deals were fake.

14
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:51,511
HBO and Netflix were not accused
of wrongdoing in these cases;

15
00:00:51,521 --> 00:00:55,790
according to the SEC, neither
company ever did business with 1inMM.

16
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:59,550
At least $650 million came in.

17
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:04,490
In 2022, a federal judge sentenced
him to 20 years in prison.

18
00:01:05,530 --> 00:01:08,242
This is the story of how
that money was rerouted,

19
00:01:08,252 --> 00:01:10,530
and the red flags you can spot next time.

20
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:16,396
Around 2013, Horwitz founded a
company called 1inMM Capital,

21
00:01:16,406 --> 00:01:21,220
which he promoted as a film distribution
company based in Los Angeles.

22
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,900
According to the SEC, the name
was short for one in a million.

23
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:30,260
According to the indictment, Horwitz
was the company's sole manager.

24
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:35,624
The pitch went like this.
Investors would put up the money,

25
00:01:35,634 --> 00:01:39,660
and 1inMM would use it to buy
distribution rights to specific films.

26
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:44,498
The company, he claimed, would then
license those rights at a profit

27
00:01:44,508 --> 00:01:47,745
to online streaming platforms
such as HBO and Netflix,

28
00:01:47,755 --> 00:01:50,340
for distribution outside the United States.

29
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:53,540
Those profits were supposed
to pay the investors back.

30
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:58,146
The SEC alleged that the films
were mostly from Latin America,

31
00:01:58,156 --> 00:02:02,477
and that the company claimed HBO,
Netflix and other media corporations

32
00:02:02,487 --> 00:02:04,220
were its Strategic Partners.

33
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:06,260
HBO and Netflix were not accused
of wrongdoing in these cases;

34
00:02:06,270 --> 00:02:08,170
according to the SEC, neither
company ever did business with 1inMM.

35
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:16,680
A promissory note is a written IOU: a
promise to repay a set amount by a set date.

36
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:21,256
In his plea agreement, Horwitz
acknowledged that 1inMM's notes were

37
00:02:21,266 --> 00:02:22,940
securities under federal law.

38
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:28,558
Each note listed the amount borrowed,
typically from about $35,000 to

39
00:02:28,568 --> 00:02:29,500
$1.5 million.

40
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:34,078
It also listed the amount
to be paid back at maturity,

41
00:02:34,088 --> 00:02:38,480
typically 6 or 12 months later:
a return of 25 to 45 percent.

42
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:43,460
In its complaint, the SEC alleged
a slightly different range:

43
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:47,880
a typical profit of 35 to 45
percent over the life of the note.

44
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:52,643
Over the life of the note
is not the same as per year,

45
00:02:52,653 --> 00:02:54,210
and that difference matters.

46
00:02:55,190 --> 00:02:58,587
In a sample of notes listed
in the SEC's complaint,

47
00:02:58,597 --> 00:03:01,670
one 6-month note promised
a 35 percent return.

48
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:06,622
If you could reinvest the whole
payout at that rate twice a year,

49
00:03:06,632 --> 00:03:10,139
you would end the year with
about 1.82 times your money:

50
00:03:10,149 --> 00:03:12,410
roughly an 82 percent annual return.

51
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:16,850
Even without reinvesting, it
works out to 70 percent a year.

52
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:20,614
When an investment
promises returns like that,

53
00:03:20,624 --> 00:03:24,202
on a fixed schedule, ask
the simplest question first:

54
00:03:24,212 --> 00:03:26,842
where would that much profit come from,

55
00:03:26,852 --> 00:03:28,070
every single time?

56
00:03:29,910 --> 00:03:34,022
According to the SEC, it was
particularly important to investors that

57
00:03:34,032 --> 00:03:37,965
HBO and Netflix were said to be
the ultimate counterparties behind

58
00:03:37,975 --> 00:03:39,050
their investments.

59
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:44,028
The complaint quotes one investor:
I believed that if HBO was

60
00:03:44,038 --> 00:03:46,250
involved, my investment was safe.

61
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:51,301
Over time, the SEC alleged,
investors were also deceived by his

62
00:03:51,311 --> 00:03:54,390
consistent track record
of paying large profits.

63
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:56,430
HBO and Netflix were not accused
of wrongdoing in these cases;

64
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:58,340
according to the SEC, neither
company ever did business with 1inMM.

65
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:04,123
The money came through
relationships. In his plea agreement,

66
00:04:04,133 --> 00:04:07,778
Horwitz admitted raising money
from at least five major groups of

67
00:04:07,788 --> 00:04:08,800
private investors.

68
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:13,381
He also admitted he understood that
those groups raised part of their

69
00:04:13,391 --> 00:04:16,780
money from sub-investors who had
no direct contract with 1inMM.

70
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:22,220
The agreement put the number of
those sub-investors at more than 250,

71
00:04:22,230 --> 00:04:24,780
a figure he reserved the right to contest.

72
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:28,940
Prosecutors said many of the
investors were his personal friends.

73
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:33,225
In other words, some of the money
came from people who had no direct

74
00:04:33,235 --> 00:04:34,340
contract with 1inMM.

75
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:39,840
Before you invest, know exactly who
is on the other side of your contract.

76
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:43,910
On paper, each note had collateral.

77
00:04:44,730 --> 00:04:48,223
An assignment of rights listed
one or more films as collateral,

78
00:04:48,233 --> 00:04:51,837
with a promise that 1inMM was the
sole and exclusive owner of the

79
00:04:51,847 --> 00:04:53,070
rights to those films.

80
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:57,575
In his plea agreement, Horwitz
admitted that 1inMM generally did not

81
00:04:57,585 --> 00:05:01,210
acquire the distribution rights
to the films listed as collateral.

82
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:04,544
And it did not enter into any
distribution agreements with the

83
00:05:04,554 --> 00:05:06,450
streaming platforms for those films.

84
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:11,253
The SEC alleged more broadly that
Horwitz and his company had no

85
00:05:11,263 --> 00:05:13,910
business relationships with Netflix or HBO,

86
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:17,070
and never signed distribution
agreements with them.

87
00:05:18,350 --> 00:05:22,050
HBO and Netflix were not accused
of wrongdoing in these cases;

88
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:26,070
according to the SEC, neither
company ever did business with 1inMM.

89
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,885
He also gave investors copies of
license agreements with sales agents

90
00:05:31,895 --> 00:05:35,920
for the films' production companies.
He admitted those agreements were fake.

91
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:40,365
He told investors that
streaming platforms had signed,

92
00:05:40,375 --> 00:05:44,243
or committed to sign, distribution
agreements with 1inMM,

93
00:05:44,253 --> 00:05:48,200
and he provided copies. Those,
he admitted, were fake too.

94
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:52,040
The notes, the collateral,
the distribution deals:

95
00:05:52,050 --> 00:05:53,600
all of it came from him.

96
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:58,044
When every piece of proof comes from
the person asking for your money,

97
00:05:58,054 --> 00:05:59,620
it is not independent proof.

98
00:06:01,530 --> 00:06:06,270
So where did the money go? Horwitz
admitted he did not use it as promised.

99
00:06:06,830 --> 00:06:09,959
Instead, it went to payments
to earlier investors,

100
00:06:09,969 --> 00:06:13,726
to keeping up the company's
facade of legitimate operations,

101
00:06:13,736 --> 00:06:18,121
to himself and entities he controlled,
and to what the agreement calls

102
00:06:18,131 --> 00:06:19,450
his lavish lifestyle.

103
00:06:20,410 --> 00:06:24,406
According to prosecutors, that
included a $6 million home,

104
00:06:24,416 --> 00:06:27,110
luxury cars, and travel by private jet.

105
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:32,645
The SEC alleged one more
detail. Around September 2017,

106
00:06:32,655 --> 00:06:37,058
it said, Horwitz falsely told an
investor group he was personally

107
00:06:37,068 --> 00:06:39,920
investing in several notes alongside them.

108
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:44,900
According to the SEC, the money he put
in had been taken from other investors.

109
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:48,359
When those notes paid out in March 2018,

110
00:06:48,369 --> 00:06:53,560
the SEC alleged, the group paid him
about $2.33 million as his share.

111
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:58,792
The SEC alleged he combined that
money with other investor funds to

112
00:06:58,802 --> 00:07:01,880
buy his home, for about
$5.7 million in cash.

113
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:06,130
When a promoter says he is
investing right alongside you,

114
00:07:06,140 --> 00:07:08,660
treat it as a claim to check, not as proof.

115
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:15,220
Paying earlier investors with money from
newer ones is the core of a Ponzi scheme.

116
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:20,520
The Justice Department called
this a $650 million Ponzi scheme.

117
00:07:21,060 --> 00:07:24,792
And the SEC alleged that those
payments persuaded investors to buy

118
00:07:24,802 --> 00:07:27,240
more notes, and to raise money from others.

119
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:32,164
That is the trap: on-time payments
feel like proof the investment

120
00:07:32,174 --> 00:07:36,080
works. In a Ponzi scheme, they
are part of how it keeps going.

121
00:07:37,930 --> 00:07:40,999
For more than five years,
the Justice Department said,

122
00:07:41,009 --> 00:07:44,830
he raised millions of dollars from
investors on these false claims.

123
00:07:45,650 --> 00:07:50,525
Then, beginning around December 2019,
1inMM began defaulting on all of

124
00:07:50,535 --> 00:07:52,070
its outstanding notes.

125
00:07:53,950 --> 00:07:57,967
Prosecutors said investors started
to complain after the company began

126
00:07:57,977 --> 00:07:59,070
defaulting in 2019.

127
00:08:00,030 --> 00:08:03,943
By the time of his plea agreement,
the company was in default on about

128
00:08:03,953 --> 00:08:05,410
$230 million in principal.

129
00:08:06,790 --> 00:08:10,288
He admitted the scheme had caused
substantial financial hardship to at

130
00:08:10,298 --> 00:08:11,350
least five investors.

131
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:16,958
The plea agreement has a word
for what came next. He acted,

132
00:08:16,968 --> 00:08:21,960
it says, to lull the investors into
believing their funds were safely invested.

133
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:26,024
Horwitz admitted he falsely
reassured investors that the missed

134
00:08:26,034 --> 00:08:28,458
payments were caused by
the streaming platforms,

135
00:08:28,468 --> 00:08:30,040
and that payments would resume.

136
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:34,792
He sent investors emails and text
messages that he claimed came from

137
00:08:34,802 --> 00:08:37,239
representatives of the platforms. In fact,

138
00:08:37,249 --> 00:08:41,026
he admitted, he had not been in
communication with the platforms,

139
00:08:41,036 --> 00:08:42,900
and the correspondence was fake.

140
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:47,942
Prosecutors later said those messages
used the identities of actual

141
00:08:47,952 --> 00:08:49,540
HBO and Netflix employees.

142
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:53,130
He had been charged with
aggravated identity theft,

143
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:55,883
but those counts were
dismissed under his plea deal,

144
00:08:55,893 --> 00:08:57,640
and he was not convicted of them.

145
00:08:57,790 --> 00:08:59,680
HBO and Netflix were not accused
of wrongdoing in these cases;

146
00:08:59,690 --> 00:09:01,590
according to the SEC, neither
company ever did business with 1inMM.

147
00:09:03,910 --> 00:09:08,687
In March 2021, according to the
SEC, he emailed some investors that

148
00:09:08,697 --> 00:09:12,270
funds would be released
by April 9, at the latest.

149
00:09:12,870 --> 00:09:16,633
The same day, the SEC alleged, he
suggested to a representative of

150
00:09:16,643 --> 00:09:20,521
another investor group that investors
could put up money for lawyers

151
00:09:20,531 --> 00:09:23,790
in the company's alleged
efforts to get payment from HBO.

152
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:29,734
In that email, according to the SEC,
he labeled the option of sharing

153
00:09:29,744 --> 00:09:32,450
that cost with investors highly doubtful.

154
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:36,701
Blaming a famous partner,
setting a firm new date,

155
00:09:36,711 --> 00:09:40,301
raising the idea of investors
putting in more money:

156
00:09:40,311 --> 00:09:43,910
each one is a reason to
slow down, not to send more.

157
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:45,950
HBO and Netflix were not accused
of wrongdoing in these cases;

158
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:47,860
according to the SEC, neither
company ever did business with 1inMM.

159
00:09:50,270 --> 00:09:57,030
On April 5, 2021, the SEC filed an
emergency case against Horwitz and 1inMM.

160
00:09:57,270 --> 00:10:00,978
The next day, the SEC announced
that the court had granted an

161
00:10:00,988 --> 00:10:02,390
emergency asset freeze.

162
00:10:03,310 --> 00:10:07,370
That same day, April 6, FBI
agents arrested Horwitz.

163
00:10:07,610 --> 00:10:12,045
He was first charged with wire
fraud. A grand jury then indicted him

164
00:10:12,055 --> 00:10:16,630
on securities fraud, wire fraud and
aggravated identity theft charges.

165
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:23,510
In October 2021, Horwitz pleaded guilty
to one count of securities fraud.

166
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:27,379
In his plea agreement, he admitted
that he was in fact guilty and

167
00:10:27,389 --> 00:10:31,427
agreed to a statement of facts.
That statement is the source of much

168
00:10:31,437 --> 00:10:33,640
of what you have heard in this story.

169
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:40,240
On February 14, 2022, U.S. District
Judge Mark C. Scarsi sentenced him

170
00:10:40,250 --> 00:10:43,940
to 240 months in federal prison: 20 years.

171
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:51,720
The judge also ordered him to
pay $230,361,884 in restitution.

172
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:54,812
The remaining charges, including wire fraud,

173
00:10:54,822 --> 00:10:57,008
were dismissed on the government's motion,

174
00:10:57,018 --> 00:10:58,640
as the plea agreement provided.

175
00:11:00,530 --> 00:11:03,827
The SEC's civil case went
on. In February 2024,

176
00:11:03,837 --> 00:11:08,259
the court entered a final judgment
against Horwitz based on his

177
00:11:08,269 --> 00:11:12,490
consent; he waived findings of
fact and any right to appeal.

178
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:16,448
That consent judgment permanently
bars him from violating the

179
00:11:16,458 --> 00:11:19,210
antifraud provisions of the
federal securities laws.

180
00:11:19,790 --> 00:11:24,540
The consent judgment also set
disgorgement and interest of about $74.2

181
00:11:24,550 --> 00:11:28,630
million, deemed satisfied by
the criminal restitution order.

182
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:34,074
Before the sentencing, prosecutors
wrote that Horwitz portrayed

183
00:11:34,084 --> 00:11:36,200
himself as a Hollywood success story.

184
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:41,198
But, they wrote, he was not a
successful businessman or Hollywood

185
00:11:41,208 --> 00:11:43,900
insider. He just played one in real life.

186
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:48,292
Prosecutors also called it the
largest known Ponzi scheme in the

187
00:11:48,302 --> 00:11:50,120
history of their federal district.

188
00:11:51,940 --> 00:11:55,462
So what can you take from
this case? Three lessons,

189
00:11:55,472 --> 00:11:57,480
five red flags, and one rule.

190
00:11:58,380 --> 00:12:02,248
Lesson one: turn a short-term
return into a yearly rate;

191
00:12:02,258 --> 00:12:06,749
a 6-month note listed in the SEC's
complaint promised 35 percent,

192
00:12:06,759 --> 00:12:08,560
roughly 70 percent a year.

193
00:12:09,460 --> 00:12:13,025
Lesson two: steady payments do
not prove an investment is real;

194
00:12:13,035 --> 00:12:16,826
in a Ponzi scheme, which is what
the Justice Department called this

195
00:12:16,836 --> 00:12:19,560
case, they are paid with
other investors' money.

196
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:24,053
Lesson three: know who is on
the other side of your contract;

197
00:12:24,063 --> 00:12:27,580
the sub-investors here had no
direct contract with 1inMM.

198
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,048
Red flag one: high, fixed
returns on a short clock,

199
00:12:33,058 --> 00:12:36,240
like 25 to 45 percent in 6 or 12 months.

200
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:39,951
Two: a famous partner you
cannot verify yourself,

201
00:12:39,961 --> 00:12:42,960
like the streaming platforms
he named to investors.

202
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:46,691
Three: all the proof comes from the seller;

203
00:12:46,701 --> 00:12:51,440
here the notes, the collateral papers
and the deal papers all came from him.

204
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:55,680
Four: an unregistered offer
that travels through friends;

205
00:12:55,690 --> 00:12:59,630
the SEC alleged that neither 1inMM
nor its securities offerings were

206
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:01,440
registered with the Commission.

207
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:04,947
Five: missed payments
blamed on someone else;

208
00:13:04,957 --> 00:13:08,522
he admitted falsely blaming the
streaming platforms for the missed

209
00:13:08,532 --> 00:13:11,240
payments, and claiming
that payments would resume.

210
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:16,113
And one rule under all of it: verify
independently before any money

211
00:13:16,123 --> 00:13:20,280
moves, including money to rescue an
investment that has stopped paying.

212
00:13:20,980 --> 00:13:24,978
To check a seller, look them up on
FINRA's BrokerCheck or the SEC's

213
00:13:24,988 --> 00:13:27,680
Investment Adviser Public
Disclosure website.

214
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:32,980
To report suspected investment
fraud, submit a tip at sec.gov;

215
00:13:32,990 --> 00:13:36,671
to report a scam to the
Federal Trade Commission,

216
00:13:36,681 --> 00:13:38,640
go to ReportFraud.ftc.gov.

217
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:42,427
The case facts in this story
come from public court and agency

218
00:13:42,437 --> 00:13:44,140
records, listed in the description.

